    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Chronic: praziquantel for 1 day (2 doses for S. haematobium, S. mansoni, S. intercalatum; 3 doses for S. japonicum, S. mekongi); it kills adult worms, not developing schistosomula or eggs, so asymptomatic travelers are treated 6-8 weeks after the last exposure."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Acute (Katayama fever; limited data): oral glucocorticoids can ease severe symptoms (low-dose prednisone for 5 days in adults is usually effective); once symptoms subside, praziquantel, repeated 4-6 weeks later."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Praziquantel adverse effects are generally mild: abdominal pain, diarrhea, headache, dizziness."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
